ve added zero conductive metal. - If you instead laid a lightweight, thin sheet of steel directly on the loop cut (maybe only 10 lbs), the loop would immediately trigger, even though the “weight” pressing on the road is only 10 lbs. So the road sensor doesn't act like a scale at all—it's more like a metal-sensing "ghost" that floats above the road and only wakes up when enough conductive material swims through its field. It never touches the car, and it never feels its weight. I completely understand why your instructor and so many others fall for the scale idea: the sensor *is* in the road, and heavier vehicles *do* tend to trigger it more reliably. But those two facts fool our brain into inventing a cause that doesn't exist. The real cause is simply metal mass and proximity.
